Below is a step‐by‐step analysis of the mission description and the resulting hierarchical goal model. In this design, we decompose the overall mission “Deliver Food to Inpatient Room” (G1) into four major sub‐goals: Meal Delivery, Meal Tracking and Alerting, Dish Retrieval, and Door Opening. Each of these sub‐goals is further decomposed according to the alternatives and fallback options described in the mission. In addition, we define concrete tasks (leaf nodes) that represent actionable steps. We use an “AND” decomposition when all children must be fulfilled and an “OR” (fallback) decomposition when fulfilling any one child is sufficient. For binary fallbacks we use the FALLBACK(…) notation; for more than two alternatives, we nest the binary fallbacks. Finally, we add custom properties (Location and Number of Robots) for each task to indicate where it is executed and how many robots are involved.

──────────────────────────────
Step 1. Top-Level Goal (G1)
──────────────────────────────
• G1 (“Deliver Food to Inpatient Room”) is a Perform goal because it requires executing several actions (delivery, tracking, dish retrieval, door opening).  
• It is decomposed into four sub‐goals (G1.1, G1.2, G1.3, G1.4) that must all succeed (AND decomposition).

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. Meal Delivery (G1.1)
──────────────────────────────
The mission states that food can be delivered in one of two ways:
 – By placing it on the room table (special manipulation required), or
 – By “fetching” it from the robot’s tray (which may require cooperation).

Because these are alternatives, we use an OR (fallback) decomposition. We prefer tray retrieval when the inpatient (or a helper) is available; otherwise, table delivery is used.  
• G1.1 is a Perform goal with runtime annotation:  
  FALLBACK(G1.1.2, G1.1.1)  
  (where G1.1.2 is “Tray Retrieval” and G1.1.1 is “Table Delivery”).

──────────────────────────────
Step 3. Meal Tracking and Alerting (G1.2)
──────────────────────────────
The system must track when and where each meal is retrieved and alert if a wrong meal is taken.  
• G1.2 is a Perform goal with sequential execution (“;”) because tracking must occur before an alert can be issued if needed.

──────────────────────────────
Step 4. Dish Retrieval (G1.3)
──────────────────────────────
Dirty dishes must be retrieved from the room. There are several methods:
 – Unassisted retrieval,
 – Cooperation between robots,
 – Cooperation with a human,
 – Or responding to a call from someone in the room.
Because any one method is acceptable, we use an OR (fallback) decomposition. For more than two alternatives, we nest the binary fallbacks.  
• G1.3 is a Perform goal with runtime annotation:  
  FALLBACK(G1.3.1, FALLBACK(G1.3.2, FALLBACK(G1.3.3, G1.3.4)))  
  (where G1.3.1 is “Unassisted Dish Retrieval”, G1.3.2 is “Robot Cooperation Dish Retrieval”, G1.3.3 is “Human Cooperation Dish Retrieval”, and G1.3.4 is “Respond to Dish Pickup Call”).

──────────────────────────────
Step 5. Door Opening (G1.4)
──────────────────────────────
The door to the inpatient room may be opened either by the robot alone or with human cooperation (when a person signals readiness).  
• G1.4 is a Perform goal with runtime annotation:  
  FALLBACK(G1.4.1, G1.4.2)  
  (where G1.4.1 is “Robot Only Door Opening” and G1.4.2 is “Human Cooperation Door Opening”).

──────────────────────────────
Step 6. Task Definitions
──────────────────────────────
Each goal is supported by one or more concrete tasks. Tasks are “leaf” nodes that include information about the location and the number of robots required. Below are the tasks derived from each goal branch.

